 in the semi-final, upset to have missed it, and for the final he convinced one of the supervisors to let him watch, alone, on a mini-laptop in the hut where the staff slept. When AndrÃ©s Iniesta scored the <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/11/world-cup-final-spain-champions">winning goal</a> in the 116th minute, he ran off screaming, doing laps of the lake. He was 14.</p><p>On the night that Spain won their second World Cup 16 years later, <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jul/13/rodri-interview-spain-captain-world-cup-2026">Rodrigo HernÃ¡ndez was back in the US</a>, not so far from there. When <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jul/20/world-cup-2026-final-spain-argentina">Ferran Torres scored </a>the winning goal in the 106th minute, he ran off screaming in a straighter line and accompanied this time: towards the corner where his other<em> family</em> were. And then, when it ended, when the fighting finished too, he collected the trophy, pausing just long enough for Donald Trump to leave centre stage before lifting it, his timing perfect even here.</p> <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jul/20/a-big-red-speaker-louis-vuitton-trophy-trunks-and-a-tattoo-bet-how-spain-celebrated-world-cup-win">Continue reading...</a>ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;JeaJ94GKp10gdj7i0iJY05ICXepYJp2z2EKgyKce4iYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;tuzqfT6ryNbqBk5TkzGhj1kcIxd8gSOaKIlt53NGqlwƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;NIBi2r0HcFd4rivOtvFwI0Gb8v9K8dJydcT9ruu5Y5sƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;4lcMGkUe8WKhvov8BrIBFzqxfP60B-pq-m_h813C45Mƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;zvLSKC8RAUjHKJ